  Next: what actually goes in the store. This decides whether the module has a known schema with typed defaults and
  validation, or is a generic key-value bag.

  [1mThe fork:[0m a fixed schema means the module owns the list of valid preference keys and their defaults, and can reject
  garbage; a generic bag means callers can store anything and the module never knows what's valid. Changing from a bag
  to a schema later means retrofitting validation onto data already in users' browsers — not cheap.

  - [1mFixed schema with defaults[0m — e.g. [38;5;153m{ rememberedUsername, theme }[39m. The module exports [38;5;153mget(key)[39m / [38;5;153mset(key, value)[39m that
    validate against a declared schema and fall back to defaults on missing or corrupt data. Buys: corrupt [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m
    can never crash the app; every caller gets a valid value. Costs: adding a preference means editing the schema.
  - [1mGeneric key-value store[0m — [38;5;153mget(key)[39m / [38;5;153mset(key, value)[39m over any JSON-serializable value, no schema. Buys: nothing to
    update when prefs are added. Costs: no defaults, no validation, and callers each have to handle [38;5;153mundefined[39m and
    corrupt data themselves.

  [1mMy recommendation: fixed schema with defaults[0m, seeded with just the preferences this app actually has a use for today.
  Concretely I'd suggest two: [38;5;153mrememberedUsername[39m (so the login form pre-fills) and [38;5;153mtheme[39m ([38;5;153mlight[39m/[38;5;153mdark[39m) —
  [38;5;153mrememberedUsername[39m is the one that makes the feature visibly do something, and [38;5;153mtheme[39m is the standard second case that
  proves the schema handles a non-string-ish value with a constrained set. To be explicit: the password is never stored,
  in any option.

  If you only want the storage layer and no wiring into the login form, say so — that's a smaller scope and I'll design
  it that way.
